<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Borromeo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1538–1584</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Carlo Borromeo, Saint Charles Borromeo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Borromeo was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1564 and a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. A nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a>, he helped bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> to its close and then enforced its decrees in his large diocese through synods, visitations, seminaries and strict discipline of the clergy. He cared for the sick during the plague of 1576–1577 and was canonised in 1610.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo.jpg" alt="Charles Borromeo" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Ambrogio Figino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlo_Borromeo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
